%I A402175 #7 Nov 25 2026 20:48:35
%S A402175 1,2,6,7,10,12,20,21,25,26,32,34,35,44,56,57,58,63,65,72,73,76,80,87,
%T A402175 88,97,103,107,108,126,130,132,143,144,145,148,161,162,167,173,188,
%U A402175 192,199,201,205,219,222,226,228,230,231,242,260,266,270,272,280,282,288
%N A402175 Numbers k such that A025487(k) can be written as a quotient in which every divisor d > 1 of A025487(k) occurs exactly once as a factor of the numerator or denominator.
%C A402175 The existence of such a quotient depends only on the prime signature (cf. A124832), so it suffices to consider the least representative of each signature, as listed in A025487 (cf. A046523).
%C A402175 Let m = A025487(k) and t = A000005(m). Then k is a term if and only if t == 2 (mod 4) or m is a fourth power, that is, m belongs to A182358 or A000583. Proof:
%C A402175 Let S be the set of divisors d > 1 placed in the denominator; the remaining divisors d > 1 form the numerator. Since A007955(m) = m^(t/2), the numerator has product m^(t/2)/prod(S). Thus the quotient equals m if and only if m^(t/2) = m*prod(S)^2, or equivalently, prod(S) = m^((t - 2)/4).
%C A402175 Necessity: Write m = p_1^e_1*...*p_r^e_r. The exponent e_i*(t - 2)/4 of p_i in prod(S) must be an integer, so 4 divides e_i*(t - 2) for every i. If t == 0 (mod 4), then t - 2 == 2 (mod 4), so all e_i would be even, forcing t to be odd, a contradiction. If t is odd, then t - 2 is odd and all e_i are divisible by 4, so m is a fourth power. The only remaining case is t == 2 (mod 4).
%C A402175 Sufficiency: If t = 4*j + 2, then m is not a square, and its divisors form 2*j + 1 complementary pairs {d, m/d}. Excluding {1, m}, choose any j of the remaining 2*j pairs and put both members of each pair into S. Then prod(S) = m^j, as required.
%C A402175 If m = q^4 > 1, then every prime exponent of m is divisible by 4, so t == 1 (mod 4), say t = 4*j + 1 with j >= 1. The divisors other than q^2 form 2*j complementary pairs. Put q^3 into S together with both members of any j - 1 of the 2*j - 2 pairs other than {1, m} and {q, q^3}. Then prod(S) = q^3*m^(j - 1) = m^((t - 2)/4), while q^2 remains in the numerator.
%C A402175 For m = 1, the empty numerator and denominator both have product 1, giving the required quotient.
%H A402175 Felix Huber, <a href="/A402175/b402175.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%e A402175 6 is a term since the divisors d > 1 of A025487(6) = 12 are 2, 3, 4, 6, 12, and 12 = (2*3*4*6)/12.
%e A402175 7 is a term since the divisors d > 1 of A025487(7) = 16 are 2, 4, 8, 16, and 16 = (2*4*16)/8.
%e A402175 4 is not a term because A025487(4) = 6 has four divisors and is not a fourth power.
%p A402175 A402175 := proc(n)
%p A402175     local b, g, s, t, u;
%p A402175     t := 1;
%p A402175     g := proc(i, e, m, d)
%p A402175         local h, p, x;
%p A402175         b[m] := evalb(irem(d, 4) = 2 or iroot(m, 4)^4 = m);
%p A402175         p := ithprime(i);
%p A402175         x := m;
%p A402175         for h to e while x*p <= t do
%p A402175             x := x*p;
%p A402175             g(i + 1, h, x, d*(h + 1));
%p A402175         end do;
%p A402175     end proc;
%p A402175     do
%p A402175         b := table();
%p A402175         g(1, t, 1, 1);
%p A402175         u := sort([indices(b, 'nolist')]);
%p A402175         s := select(x -> b[x], u);
%p A402175         if nops(s) >= n then
%p A402175             return nops(select(x -> x <= s[n], u));
%p A402175         end if;
%p A402175         t := 2*t;
%p A402175     end do;
%p A402175 end proc:
%p A402175 seq(A402175(n), n = 1 .. 59);
%Y A402175 Cf. A000005, A000583, A007955, A025487, A046523, A124832, A182358, A402176.
%K A402175 nonn,new
%O A402175 1,2
%A A402175 _Felix Huber_, Nov 19 2026
